  {
   "id": "sched",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 5: why so many warps? Because memory is slow. Suppose each warp loads a value, then adds using it, and a load takes 4 cycles."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 5 of 7: hiding latency"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "map"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "ws"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ws[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "With one warp, the add must wait for the load. 6 of these 10 cycles issue nothing."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ws[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "With four warps, while warp 0 waits, warps 1, 2 and 3 issue their loads. By cycle 5, warp 0's data has arrived. Every cycle is used."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ws[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "This is fine-grained multithreading, from Module 3, at a huge scale. The number of warps needed is about 1 plus the latency divided by the work between long waits."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "ws"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "wsx"
      },
